---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Showcase from "../../../components/Showcase.astro";
import StylingHooks from "../../../components/StylingHooks.astro";
import { InputBasicDemo } from "../../../components/react-demos/input";



const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/react`;

const css = `@import "@skryensya/core/tokens.scss";
@import "@skryensya/core/components/input.css";
@import "@skryensya/core/components/field.css";`;

const html = `<div class="sk-field">
  <label class="sk-field__label" for="email">
    Email
    <span class="sk-field__required" aria-hidden="true">*</span>
  </label>
  <div class="sk-field__hint" id="email-hint">
    We only use it for ballots.
  </div>

  <input
    class="sk-input"
    id="email"
    name="email"
    type="email"
    required
    aria-describedby="email-hint email-error"
    aria-invalid="true"
  />

  <div class="sk-field__error" id="email-error">
    Enter a work address.
  </div>
</div>`;

const react = `import { Field, Input, Textarea } from "@skryensya/react/input";

function Example() {
  return (
    <>
      <Field
        label="Email"
        hint="We only use it for ballots."
        error={errors.email}
        required
      >
        <Input name="email" type="email" />
      </Field>

      <Field label="Notes">
        <Textarea name="notes" />
      </Field>
    </>
  );
}`;
---

<Base title="Input" description="Input: native control, Field with label, hint, error, and styling hooks.">
  <h1>Input and Field</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The native text control stays native: there is no machine, and there is no border <code>div</code> posing as input. The platform is left with the validation, the autofill, the IME and the association to the form.
  </p>

  <Showcase html={html} react={react} label="Input">
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <InputBasicDemo client:load />
    </Fragment>
  </Showcase>
  <p>Density compacts the space around the field, not its interaction area: Input retains a minimum of <code>44px</code> high, even with <code>data-size="sm"</code>.</p>

  <h2>A class for all text controls</h2>
  <p>
    <code>sk-input</code> goes in <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> and in <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code>: it is the same visual control, so it is a single set of hooks. A second class would be a second set to keep in sync with the first.
  </p>

  <h2>Field is independent of Input</h2>
  <p>
    <code>sk-field</code> is the chrome around <em>any</em> control, it wraps a select or a group of checkboxes just as well. Naming it after the control it holds most often would make it a lie the first time it holds another.
  </p>

  <h2>The error is text, not a color</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--sk-field-error-fg</code> colors a message that must exist, and <code>aria-invalid</code>, which you already write for screen readers, is what the <code>--sk-input-border-color</code> hook follows. A separate <code>data-invalid</code> would allow the two to not match, and color is never the only sign of error (WCAG 1.4.1).
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>CSS</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={css} label="CSS" />


  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>
    In React the <code>Field</code> does the wiring that you write above by hand: generates the <code>id</code>, arms the <code>aria-describedby</code> of the hint and the error, and passes <code>required</code> and <code>disabled</code> to the native control. The presence of <code>error</code> is what makes the field invalid. A <code>Input</code> outside of a <code>Field</code> is still a valid control.
  </p>
  <p>The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of the preview.</p>
  <StylingHooks groups={[{"file":"components/input.css","probe":"sk-input","title":"Input"},{"file":"components/field.css","probe":"sk-field","title":"Field"}]} />
</Base>
